WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.203
Welcome to Module 6 of Global Secure
Access as the Identity-Aware Network:

2
00:00:05.203 --> 00:00:08.075
rings, rollback and the evidence package.

3
00:00:08.075 --> 00:00:11.450
Modules 1 to 5 built
every piece for a pilot.

4
00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:15.687
This module makes it a migration, and
then hands an assessor the evidence.

5
00:00:15.687 --> 00:00:20.634
It covers Steps 11 and 12 of the Global
Secure Access implementation guide,

6
00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:24.703
with its RACI, evidence catalog,
metrics, dangers and rollback,

7
00:00:24.703 --> 00:00:30.250
and takes about forty minutes, plus a lab
that rolls your whole test tenant back.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.150
Why does this matter?

9
00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.013
A pilot isn't a deployment.

10
00:00:37.013 --> 00:00:41.531
In this module the pilot becomes a
migration: users move ring by ring,

11
00:00:41.531 --> 00:00:46.373
each ring exits on criteria you measure,
the V P N route for an application

12
00:00:46.373 --> 00:00:51.537
goes only after its wave holds, and anything
can be rolled back in a known order.

13
00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:56.269
Then each network finding gets an
evidence package an assessor can check:

14
00:00:56.269 --> 00:01:00.924
configuration, test, operational,
exception, and reassessment evidence.

15
00:01:00.924 --> 00:01:03.964
The assessor recommends a
finding ready for closure,

16
00:01:03.964 --> 00:01:06.175
and the authorizing official decides.

17
00:01:06.175 --> 00:01:09.426
In the lab, you rehearse the
rollback on your test tenant,

18
00:01:09.426 --> 00:01:13.250
and keep the exports that show
the state before and after it.

19
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.913
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

20
00:01:19.913 --> 00:01:21.498
You'll run the four rings,

21
00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:25.937
with exit criteria and backout
triggers set before each one starts.

22
00:01:25.937 --> 00:01:30.040
You'll keep the V P N, DirectAccess
and another security service edge

23
00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:34.288
working beside the service during
the move, and explain branch parity.

24
00:01:34.288 --> 00:01:38.126
You'll say who is responsible and
accountable for each activity,

25
00:01:38.126 --> 00:01:39.574
and who decides closure.

26
00:01:39.574 --> 00:01:43.350
You'll assemble the five kinds of
evidence for each network finding.

27
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.712
And you'll compute the guide's coverage
metrics, and roll back in the guide's order.

28
00:01:47.712 --> 00:01:50.229
You need Modules 1 to 5 and their labs,

29
00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.675
and Zero Trust and Assessment,
chapter ten, for the closure chain.

30
00:01:54.675 --> 00:01:58.190
The lab runs in a test
tenant, never production.

31
00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:03.502
Unit 1 is the rings.

32
00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:07.077
Ring zero is the implementing
team; it exits when every

33
00:02:07.077 --> 00:02:11.128
step's Validate has passed and
break-glass has been rehearsed.

34
00:02:11.128 --> 00:02:15.663
Ring one is the pilot, fewer than
twenty users across roles and sites,

35
00:02:15.663 --> 00:02:19.794
for ten business days, with no
unresolved severity-two ticket,

36
00:02:19.794 --> 00:02:24.491
the client active on every pilot
device, and clean report-only results.

37
00:02:24.491 --> 00:02:28.452
Ring two is about ten percent
of users, including one branch,

38
00:02:28.452 --> 00:02:32.334
for twenty business days, with
help desk volume within the

39
00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:36.215
agreed threshold and a T L S
failure rate under one percent.

40
00:02:36.215 --> 00:02:42.052
Ring three is everyone in scope, in waves
by organization, each wave meeting ring

41
00:02:42.052 --> 00:02:44.970
two's criteria, and each application's V P

42
00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.977
N route removed only after its wave holds.

43
00:02:47.977 --> 00:02:53.615
Around every ring: the emergency access
group is excluded from every policy in Steps

44
00:02:53.615 --> 00:02:58.770
5 to 10 and tested the day each ring
starts; backout triggers are set in advance;

45
00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:03.281
and, as Learn categorizes changes,
enabling a new traffic profile or

46
00:03:03.281 --> 00:03:09.030
restructuring connector groups is a major
change for the change advisory board.

47
00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.805
During the move, everything
runs side by side.

48
00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:19.237
The V P N stays for
applications not yet published,

49
00:03:19.237 --> 00:03:22.166
in split-include mode
where it supports it,

50
00:03:22.166 --> 00:03:26.435
so Private Access and the V P N
don't contend for the same routes;

51
00:03:26.435 --> 00:03:29.867
a V P N route is removed only
after the V P N's own logs

52
00:03:29.867 --> 00:03:33.968
show no traffic for that
application for ten business days.

53
00:03:33.968 --> 00:03:38.468
DirectAccess can't coexist with
Private Access on the same device.

54
00:03:38.468 --> 00:03:42.906
Where a proxy is set at the
operating system or browser level,

55
00:03:42.906 --> 00:03:48.143
Learn's P A C file guidance excludes
every F Q D N and I P the client tunnels.

56
00:03:48.143 --> 00:03:52.542
A third-party security service edge
kept for internet traffic follows

57
00:03:52.542 --> 00:03:56.643
its coexistence page on Learn, for
the bypasses in both directions.

58
00:03:56.643 --> 00:04:01.944
And branches: a remote network carries
the Microsoft and internet profiles, not

59
00:04:01.944 --> 00:04:07.405
Private Access, and applies only the baseline
profile, with no Conditional Access.

60
00:04:07.405 --> 00:04:10.645
That's a parity gap under
tick's Branch Office use case,

61
00:04:10.645 --> 00:04:14.327
so the guide keeps the client
on managed devices at branches,

62
00:04:14.327 --> 00:04:17.850
and uses the remote network
for devices without one.

63
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.463
Unit 2 is who does what.

64
00:04:23.463 --> 00:04:28.958
Microsoft's operations guide gives a RACI
with a service owner, network security

65
00:04:28.958 --> 00:04:34.125
engineer, identity engineer, S O C analyst,
help desk and platform operations.

66
00:04:34.125 --> 00:04:37.661
The guide maps those to six
roles and adds the assessor,

67
00:04:37.661 --> 00:04:39.625
who is independent of the work.

68
00:04:39.625 --> 00:04:43.393
The policy owner, the service
owner with the I S S O consulted,

69
00:04:43.393 --> 00:04:48.467
is accountable for the decision record,
the architecture, the traffic profiles,

70
00:04:48.467 --> 00:04:52.004
the Conditional Access
policies and their exclusions,

71
00:04:52.004 --> 00:04:55.387
the T L S inspection scope,
and the exception register.

72
00:04:55.387 --> 00:04:59.018
The platform owner, the network
security and identity engineers,

73
00:04:59.018 --> 00:05:00.900
is responsible for building them.

74
00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:06.088
Operators run the client rings, the
connectors and the domain controller sensors.

75
00:05:06.088 --> 00:05:10.426
The security monitor owns alerts,
log freshness and the metrics.

76
00:05:10.426 --> 00:05:13.175
The help desk takes tier-one user issues.

77
00:05:13.175 --> 00:05:17.802
The assessor assesses the evidence and
recommends a finding ready for closure,

78
00:05:17.802 --> 00:05:22.500
and the authorizing official decides, or
the assessor for an assessment finding.

79
00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.070
Learn's advice is at least
two people for every role.

80
00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.207
What goes in the package?

81
00:05:31.207 --> 00:05:34.070
The guide's evidence
catalog has five kinds.

82
00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:38.346
Configuration: the hashed Graph
export, rerun before every change,

83
00:05:38.346 --> 00:05:43.245
plus the decision and architecture
records and the diagnostic setting GET.

84
00:05:43.245 --> 00:05:46.635
Test: each Validate record,
expected and observed,

85
00:05:46.635 --> 00:05:50.934
from the client health check
through the source I P comparison,

86
00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:55.234
the blocked category, the port
tests, the disable-and-retry test,

87
00:05:55.234 --> 00:05:58.458
the four cases, the T L S
issuer, and the klist test.

88
00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:02.146
Operational: the queries,
run on the day-two cadence.

89
00:06:02.146 --> 00:06:06.417
Exception: the register, with
every exclusion and bypass,

90
00:06:06.417 --> 00:06:08.507
its approver and its expiry.

91
00:06:08.507 --> 00:06:12.713
And reassessment: the Zero Trust
Assessment rerun after each ring,

92
00:06:12.713 --> 00:06:16.307
with each network finding's
closure chain entry updated.

93
00:06:16.307 --> 00:06:20.880
A finding is ready for closure when
every applicable control state,

94
00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:25.533
designed, configured, enforced,
evidenced and operated, has evidence.

95
00:06:25.533 --> 00:06:30.226
And a Zero Trust Assessment test skipped
because the tenant has no Internet

96
00:06:30.226 --> 00:06:35.280
Access or Private Access license is a
licensing result, not a failed control.

97
00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:39.905
Unit 3.

98
00:06:39.905 --> 00:06:44.067
The metrics measure protected
coverage, not whether a setting exists.

99
00:06:44.067 --> 00:06:47.543
Client coverage, ninety-eight
percent after ring three.

100
00:06:47.543 --> 00:06:50.592
Sign-ins through the service,
ninety-five percent.

101
00:06:50.592 --> 00:06:54.455
Compliant network enforcement, a
hundred percent after ring three.

102
00:06:54.455 --> 00:06:59.930
The Quick Access residual, and apps
without an assignment or policy, both zero.

103
00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.520
The T L S failure share under one percent,

104
00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.580
with more than ninety
days of certificate life.

105
00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:08.292
And no table silent for twenty-four hours.

106
00:07:08.292 --> 00:07:09.517
Then the rollback.

107
00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:13.958
In an incident that touches several
steps, reverse newest first:

108
00:07:13.958 --> 00:07:18.481
Conditional Access policies from
Steps 10, 7, 6 and 5 to report-only;

109
00:07:18.481 --> 00:07:20.994
the security profile links removed;

110
00:07:20.994 --> 00:07:24.596
universal tenant restrictions
tagging turned off;

111
00:07:24.596 --> 00:07:30.040
the affected groups removed from the
traffic profiles; the V P N route restored;

112
00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:34.480
and signaling turned off last, only
after no policy depends on it.

113
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.222
Every rollback is a change with a record,

114
00:07:37.222 --> 00:07:42.400
followed by the Step 2.1 export, so
the evidence shows the state after it.

115
00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:50.975
The lab runs in a test tenant, never
production, and the authors haven't run it.

116
00:07:50.975 --> 00:07:53.650
It removes what Modules 1 to 5 built.

117
00:07:53.650 --> 00:07:57.962
Step one measures ring one's
exit criteria: in the workspace,

118
00:07:57.962 --> 00:08:03.331
run Step 4's sign-in share, Step 9's T L
S outcomes and Step 12's freshness query,

119
00:08:03.331 --> 00:08:08.435
and write the ring exit record, each
criterion with its measured value,

120
00:08:08.435 --> 00:08:12.132
from these queries, Module
2's device usage summary,

121
00:08:12.132 --> 00:08:15.388
and the report-only
results of Modules 3 and 4.

122
00:08:15.388 --> 00:08:19.663
A table with no rows in twenty-four
hours fails the freshness metric.

123
00:08:19.663 --> 00:08:23.909
Step two takes the evidence
export, the guide's Step 2.1,

124
00:08:23.909 --> 00:08:27.962
into a dated ring-one folder
with its hash manifest.

125
00:08:27.962 --> 00:08:30.903
Step three lists the I
D's the rollback needs:

126
00:08:30.903 --> 00:08:34.824
the four test policies and
the three forwarding profiles.

127
00:08:34.824 --> 00:08:39.112
And step four starts the
rollback in the guide's order:

128
00:08:39.112 --> 00:08:44.620
each Conditional Access policy to
report-only, Steps 10, 7, 6, then 5.

129
00:08:48.370 --> 00:08:51.558
Step five removes the
security profile's links.

130
00:08:51.558 --> 00:08:55.058
The T L S inspection policy
comes out in Learn's order:

131
00:08:55.058 --> 00:09:00.432
unlink it from the pilot profile, delete
the policy, then remove the certificate.

132
00:09:00.432 --> 00:09:03.308
The filtering policy's Conditional Access

133
00:09:03.308 --> 00:09:06.183
policy goes to disabled,
the Step 5 rollback.

134
00:09:06.183 --> 00:09:09.362
Step six turns off tenant
restrictions tagging;

135
00:09:09.362 --> 00:09:12.383
the tenant restrictions
policy itself stays.

136
00:09:12.383 --> 00:09:16.220
Step seven takes the groups out
of each profile's assignments

137
00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:19.532
in the admin center, then
disables the three profiles.

138
00:09:19.532 --> 00:09:24.182
In production, the next line of the
rollback is restoring the V P N route.

139
00:09:24.182 --> 00:09:29.494
Step eight turns off signaling, last,
now that no policy enforces on it.

140
00:09:29.494 --> 00:09:31.641
Step nine takes the export again,

141
00:09:31.641 --> 00:09:36.650
so the evidence shows the state after
the rollback: three profiles disabled,

142
00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:40.545
signaling and tagging disabled,
and no test policy enabled.

143
00:09:40.545 --> 00:09:45.534
Steps ten to twelve clean up: delete
the test policies, apps, profile and

144
00:09:45.534 --> 00:09:50.695
filtering policy; remove the QUIC
rule; and delete the telemetry setting.

145
00:09:50.695 --> 00:09:56.320
In production, that last one ends the
evidence trail and happens only by change.

146
00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:00.240
Keep the lab folder; it's
the path's evidence package.

147
00:10:03.990 --> 00:10:06.601
The federal requirement, from NIST special

148
00:10:06.601 --> 00:10:09.865
publication eight hundred
fifty-three, revision five.

149
00:10:09.865 --> 00:10:13.507
The rings support C M three,
C M four and C P two:

150
00:10:13.507 --> 00:10:18.565
each wave moved with exit
criteria, rollback and validation.

151
00:10:18.565 --> 00:10:23.690
The evidence is the ring exit records, with
the criteria and their measured values.

152
00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:28.684
The telemetry supports A U two,
A U six, A U twelve and S I four:

153
00:10:28.684 --> 00:10:33.890
network logs exported, fresh,
reviewed, and shared as agreed.

154
00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.536
The evidence is the
diagnostic setting GET,

155
00:10:36.536 --> 00:10:39.990
the freshness result, and
the CISA telemetry agreement.

156
00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:45.356
Step 12's logging is mandated in part, by
O M B memo M twenty-six fourteen and tick

157
00:10:45.356 --> 00:10:50.890
three point oh's telemetry requirement;
the specific measures are the program's.

158
00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:54.140
Each row lines up with
fed-ramp twenty X indicators.

159
00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:58.290
The evidence supports the
controls; the assessor decides.

160
00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.015
Check yourself with two questions.

161
00:11:04.015 --> 00:11:08.778
First: when is the V P N route for
a migrated application removed?

162
00:11:08.778 --> 00:11:12.701
The answer is b: only after the
wave holds, with the V P N's own

163
00:11:12.701 --> 00:11:17.377
logs showing no traffic for that
application for ten business days.

164
00:11:17.377 --> 00:11:20.739
Second: in the guide's
rollback, what comes last?

165
00:11:20.739 --> 00:11:25.711
The answer is c: disabling signaling,
only after no policy depends on it;

166
00:11:25.711 --> 00:11:28.453
turning it off earlier can block users.

167
00:11:28.453 --> 00:11:33.730
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

168
00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:38.305
To sum up.

169
00:11:38.305 --> 00:11:43.112
You measured a ring's exit criteria, took
the evidence export before the change,

170
00:11:43.112 --> 00:11:47.328
rolled the test tenant back in the
guide's order, took the export again

171
00:11:47.328 --> 00:11:51.617
to show the state after the rollback,
and removed the lab's test objects.

172
00:11:51.617 --> 00:11:54.379
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

173
00:11:54.379 --> 00:11:58.060
the ring exit record with each
criterion's measured value,

174
00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:01.740
the ring one and rolled-back
exports with their manifests,

175
00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:05.268
the rollback change record,
and the exception register.

176
00:12:05.268 --> 00:12:10.198
In production, add the CISA telemetry
agreement and the Zero Trust Assessment

177
00:12:10.198 --> 00:12:15.054
rerun after each ring, with each network
finding's closure chain entry updated.

178
00:12:15.054 --> 00:12:16.555
That completes the path.

179
00:12:16.555 --> 00:12:21.470
From here, work through the guide's
implementation checklist, one ring at a time.
